# nss -- Network Security Services, and the last package between this chain and
# evolution-data-server.
#
# Here because e-d-s hard-requires it and NOTHING AVOIDS THAT. e-d-s's
# cmake/modules/FindSMIME.cmake opens by documenting -DENABLE_SMIME=OFF as the
# way to skip NSS, and that option IS honoured -- for the CODE, through
# config.h and #ifdef blocks in src/camel. It is never consulted by the SEARCH:
# the module tries pkg-config for nspr/nss unconditionally and falls through to
# a manual hunt ending in FATAL_ERROR. The flag disables the feature and not the
# requirement. Upstream has known since bug 792746 ("Fails to build with
# ENABLE_SMIME set to OFF"), so do not retry that flag believing the header.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://archive.mozilla.org/pub/security/nss/releases/NSS_3_115_RTM/src/nss-3.115.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=ac2a47fb33bd79320159144e01c0d4af9a937a2d928c7c77ff06f5d9507861ab
SRC_DIR=nss-3.115

# THE PATCH IS NOT COSMETIC AND DOES TWO SEPARATE LOAD-BEARING THINGS. Read
# whole rather than trusted by its name; patches/0001-nss-standalone.patch is
# BLFS 12.4's nss-standalone-1, upstream sha256
# 87bb1af0b11fd41311b9899187f6e4b3fca9940651123c7bc836ec7497d2da84, recorded as
# provenance rather than as a fetch guard since the file is committed here.
#
#   1. IT CREATES nss.pc AND nss-config, WHICH NSS DOES NOT SHIP AT ALL. The
#      patch adds nss/config/{Makefile,nss.pc.in,nss-config.in} and appends
#      `config` to DIRS in manifest.mn so they are built. Without it there is no
#      pkg-config file anywhere, and e-d-s's pkg_check_modules for nss finds
#      nothing -- against an NSS that installed its libraries perfectly.
#
#   2. IT REMOVES `$(MAKE) build_nspr` FROM nss/Makefile. Upstream NSS expects
#      NSPR as a SIBLING SOURCE TREE and builds it as a step. This tree has NSPR
#      as a system package, so unpatched NSS would go looking for
#      ../nspr/<objdir>/config.status and fail. That is the "standalone" in the
#      name.
#
# THERE IS NO CONFIGURE. This is a raw make build, which means A MISSPELLED
# VARIABLE IS SILENTLY IGNORED -- no unknown-option warning, no error, just a
# different build. Every variable below therefore has its outcome asserted in
# install.sh rather than its spelling trusted:
#
#   BUILD_OPT=1              optimized build; without it NSS builds debug, which
#                            is slower and larger and looks identical afterwards
#   NSPR_INCLUDE_DIR=/usr/include/nspr
#                            the literal path nspr's recipe exists to guarantee
#                            -- see pkgs/nspr/prepare.sh, which disables NSPR's
#                            RELEASE variable so its headers land exactly there
#   USE_SYSTEM_ZLIB=1 ZLIB_LIBS=-lz
#                            link the packaged zlib instead of NSS's bundled
#                            copy. Asserted by DT_NEEDED, because a bundled
#                            static zlib leaves no trace in the link line
#   NSS_ENABLE_WERROR=0      NSS builds -Werror by default and its own code does
#                            not survive newer GCC. This is not payload
#                            avoidance; with it on, the build fails
#   USE_64=1                 x86_64 ONLY -- same asymmetry as nspr's
#                            --enable-64bit, and for the same reason
#   NSS_USE_SYSTEM_SQLITE=1  when sqlite3.h is present, which it is: sqlite is
#                            packaged. Also asserted by DT_NEEDED
#
# There is no `make install` either -- the install is a hand-written copy out of
# dist/. See install.sh.
